<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Georgia</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Tbilisi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The independent republic of Georgia on the Black Sea, heir of ancient Kartli and Colchis, whose path since 1991 has been shaped by conflict with Russia and Western ambitions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Georgia declared independence from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> on 9 April 1991, two years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> massacre, restoring the legacy of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-georgia">Democratic Republic of Georgia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zviad-gamsakhurdia">Zviad Gamsakhurdia</a> became president, but he was ousted in a civil war in the winter of 1991-1992, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-shevardnadze">Eduard Shevardnadze</a> returned to lead the country.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-ossetia">South Ossetia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-abkhazia">Abkhazia</a> in 1991-1993 left both regions outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi's</a> control. The 2003 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rose-revolution">Rose Revolution</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikheil-saakashvili">Mikheil Saakashvili</a> to power, who fought corruption and sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and EU membership. The 2008 war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> recognising both breakaway regions. Georgia's economy rests on transit, agriculture, wine and tourism.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The Georgian Dream party, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidzina-ivanishvili">Bidzina Ivanishvili</a>, has governed since 2012. Georgia gained EU candidate status in 2023, but disputes over democracy and relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> brought mass protests in 2024.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/georgia.jpg" alt="Georgia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Last update by MapGrid SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Georgia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tbilisi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zviad Gamsakhurdia, Eduard Shevardnadze, Mikheil Saakashvili</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Georgia, Sakartvelo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=georgia" aria-label="Georgia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5771 691 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Georgia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6091 947L6091 947M6091 947L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6091 947h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tbilisi. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=georgia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
